  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121749,
      "SummaryText": "Red Star Yeast Company LLC facility in rural Headland, Alabama\u0097in the Dothan metropolitan area \u0097produces liquid and dry yeast products.  The company is dedicated to providing a safe and healthy work environment for all its employees and protecting the environment and the communities where we operate. Our priority is to prevent accidental releases of hazardous materials instead of merely responding to problems.\nThe facility uses anhydrous ammonia combined with water to generate 19.4% aqueous ammonia which is used for pH control in the yeast fermentation process. The anhydrous ammonia tank and mixing system is a regulated EPA RMP process (40 CFR 68). The aqueous ammonia storage and feed systems are not regulated because they are below the 20% regulated concentration threshold but the facility has procedures for safely operating these systems to meet its general duty obligations for Accidental Release Prevention.\nAccidental Release Prevention Steps\nThe ammonia system was installed in accordance with our local building and fire codes, as well as industry consensus codes and standards. We have implemented an effective release prevention program encompassing the twelve prevention elements of both EPA RMP and OSHA Process Safety Management requirements (29 CFR 1910.119) to prevent or minimize accidental releases of ammonia. These include safety controls (alarms, detection and automatic shutdown devices) to identify and prevent potentially unsafe conditions; a preventive maintenance program designed to maintain the on-going integrity of the process equipment; a training program designed to ensure that the systems are operated by qualified personnel; and emergency action plans and procedures that enable trained personnel to respond quickly to system upsets.\nFive-Year Accident History\nAs an indication of the effectiveness of the above-mentioned programs, the site has experienced no accidental releases of ammonia within the past five years that resulted in, injuries, property"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121749,
      "SummaryText": "damage, evacuations, or environmental damage. \nEmergency Response\nEmergency Response to any potential incident at the plant is accomplished in accordance with the site's emergency action program which includes procedures describing how the facility will respond to ammonia emergencies, evacuation procedures and instructions for contacting outside responders for assistance. The program has been coordinated with local fire department officials and a copy of the site\u0092s emergency plan has been sent to the LEPC. In the event of an off-site release, County Emergency Management will provide emergency response support and any evacuation / shelter-in-place notifications to the general public that may be needed.\nPlanned Changes to Improve Safety\nThrough our PSM/RMP program, we have already installed state-of-the art detection and protective systems and continually work to safely manage chemical hazards to protect our employees and the community. Each year we will review procedures, maintain equipment, follow safe work practices and coordinate with community emergency responders. Periodically, we will audit our program, update our Process Hazard Analysis and implement any additional improvements recommended by these evaluations. Based on our most recent evaluation, we continue to implement appropriate process and procedural safety improvements on an ongoing basis."
    }
  ]
